-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell I16: the SILENT corner of the chain axis. `Sizeable` is nameable
-- here only through `reexp`'s `export import ifc.{Sizeable}`, exactly as in i14 -- but the
-- impl and the standalone share the receiver head (String), so BOTH candidates typecheck
-- and the shadow decision is observable ONLY as a value: 7 (the impl) if `size` is a
-- shadow here, 99 (the standalone) if it is not. Per S1 the interface IS nameable through
-- the chain, so `size` is a shadow, `String` has a live impl, and S2's importer rule
-- dispatches: 7.
--
-- ⚠️ PAIRED WITH `direct.mdk`, graded as its own row: the same graph with the chain hop
-- removed. They must agree, and here agreement is about a NUMBER rather than a verdict.
--
-- WHY THIS CELL EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM i14. i14 covers the chain axis with a receiver the
-- standalone cannot accept, so a lost shadow shows up as a located REJECT -- loud, and
-- impossible to miss. This corpus's own history is that the LOUD half of a defect gets a
-- fixture and the silent half does not (d18/d21, i6/i7), and the silent half is the one
-- that ships. Here a lost shadow prints 99 at exit 0 on all three verbs with no
-- diagnostic, and the ONLY thing that can catch it is a pinned value.
import reexp.{Sizeable}
import fmod.{size}

main = println (size "abc")
